  "text": "Years went the way years go in Skerrin, which is to say nobody counted them.\n\nMaren's knees learned the eleventh step before her feet did. The rail was still cold in summer. She still did not light the lamp.\n\nErris Kessock had stopped asking the time. She had stopped swinging her heels on the wall, too, and then one evening she had simply been at the lighthouse door when Maren got there, and had followed her up, and had stood at the back of the lamp-room with her hands in her pockets, saying nothing. She came the next night. After that it was not worth remarking on.\n\nThe log had a second column of hands now. Agnes's, cramped and certain. Maren's. And below those, lately, a third that was not a Holt's at all, written in because Erris had asked whether she might, and Maren had not known any reason to say no.\n\nOn a night like the others, Maren took off her left glove and put it in her pocket and did not reach for the key.\n\n\"It's warm,\" she said. \"From my coat.\"\n\nErris took it. She found the keyhole by touch. The clock was where it was.\n\nThey waited for the minute hand together, two shapes in the dark with the harbour lying black and proper in the window behind them.\n\nAt ten past, the girl turned it once, and twice, and three times, and let go.\n\nThe clock went on. Maren listened for a while to be sure. Then she went down ahead of her, so she could hear the door stick."
